2. Description
The Power BI application does not appear to adequately restrict workspace metadata and resource access based on the authenticated user's assigned entitlements.

During testing, the authenticated user was assigned access to the WMPT_LENDING_QA workspace only. However, by intercepting the request to /powerbi/metadata/refreshermetadata and inspecting its response, details of additional workspaces were returned, including MSHL_Executive_Workspace_QA and WMPT_LENDING_DEV.

The response disclosed workspace names, artifact IDs, and group object IDs associated with these workspaces.

Additionally, by modifying the workspace/folder object ID in the Power BI URL, the tester was able to access reports and semantic models belonging to other workspaces outside the assigned workspace entitlement.

This indicates that workspace-level authorization may not be consistently enforced when retrieving workspace metadata and accessing associated Power BI resources.
3. Impact
An authenticated user with access to one authorized workspace may potentially access information belonging to other business units or teams.

Potential security impacts include:
- Unauthorized disclosure of reports and dashboard information.
- Exposure of semantic model metadata and business information.
- Access to reports belonging to other business units.
- Exposure of internal workspace identifiers and resource details.
- Potential exposure of sensitive business data through reports and model access.
- Increased risk of unauthorized data extraction if export or underlying-data permissions are also available.
If write permissions are also improperly enforced, unauthorized users may potentially modify or manipulate resources belonging to other teams. This should be validated separately and should not be claimed as demonstrated by read-only access.
